{"name":"Warm Alpaca","hex":"#cdb49f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"warm","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a somewhat high temperature, often but not always connoting that the high temperature is pleasant rather than uncomfortable.","Friendly and with affection."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make or keep warm.","To become warm, to heat up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/warm"},{"term":"alpaca","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sheep-like domesticated animal of the Andes, Vicugna pacos, in the camel family, closely related to the llama, guanaco, and vicuña.","Wool from the alpaca, with strong very long fibres and coloring from black to brown to white."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/alpaca"}],"entries":[{"title":"Alpaca","blocks":[{"text":"The alpaca is a domesticated species of South American camelid. Traditionally, alpacas were kept in herds that grazed on the level heights of the Andes of Southern Peru, Western Bolivia, Ecuador, and Northern Chile. More recently, alpacas may be found on farms and ranches worldwide, with thousands of animals born and raised annually. Alpacas are especially popular in North America, Europe, and Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Alpaca_in_Higashiyama_Zoo_-_1.jpg/330px-Alpaca_in_Higashiyama_Zoo_-_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpaca","wikidata":"Q122181742"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Leefgebied_alpaca.JPG/330px-Leefgebied_alpaca.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Leefgebied_alpaca.JPG/330px-Leefgebied_alpaca.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeefgebied_